# Every text file is stored with LF line endings, whatever the platform it was written on.
#
# Without this, a checkout on Windows with core.autocrlf off commits CRLF, and a file that changed
# three lines shows as rewritten in full: the nearby-sharing work arrived with fifteen such files,
# including app/build.gradle.kts, where a 17-line change read as 315. It also keeps the Kotlin and
# JavaScript test vectors byte-identical on every machine, which the cross-language test relies on.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Batch files are the one thing Windows needs in its own convention.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Binary, whatever a heuristic might guess.
*.ftree binary
*.jks binary
